desc:Hierophant [Arcana drums] (GFX)
//tags: instrument drum machine drums sequencer generative pattern generator euclidean odd meter polyrhythm midi arcana
//author: GardenFX
// by JGWizrad / Garden FX (GFX)
//
// Requires gfx-drift, gfx-output, gfx-ui, gfx-panel, gfx-instrument and gfx-clip (.jsfx-inc) in the same folder.
//
// THE HIEROPHANT (card V of the GFX Arcana series): tradition, ritual, the keeper of the patterns. A generative drum
// machine: three engines write the rhythm (and pick the kit that plays it) in any meter and grouping, eight lanes, fills
// at the bar's end, every choice drawn from a SEED, so a pattern - and a whole render - repeats exactly. It plays its
// own synthesised kit (no samples) and sends the pattern out as MIDI on a General MIDI drum map, either or both.
//   ENGINE     lit keys; each sets how the lanes are written, the groove's character and the kit:
//              PCM12  a tight, quantised grid in the vintage drum-machine topologies (kick on the strong steps and
//                     anticipations, the backbeat, eighth hats, the clap doubling the last backbeat, a cymbal only
//                     after a fill); two or three fixed velocities. Its kit: GFX PCM12's own - its sound ROM (synthesised
//                     once at 40 kHz) through its playback hardware: clock-tuned channels holding each value (drawn
//                     band-limited), a VCA after the converter, velocity in 10 steps (the top one the accent: a harder
//                     attack, a longer decay, a faster clock), a retrigger ramp, the bus crosstalk, the 4-pole output
//                     filter and the soft output stage; on its default TIGHT FUNK kit: BD 1, SD TIGHT, HH CLOSED / OPEN,
//                     CLAP, TOM 3 (low), TOM 1 (high), COWBELL - and its CRASH for the hit after a fill. ERA (a drop-down
//                     under the keys): its 12-bit linear converter or the early 8-bit companded one.
//              DEEP   sub-surface: generative ghost notes on the snare, sixteenth hats on a probabilistic velocity
//                     curve, a dotted (three-against-two) layer in the kick, rim and low tom, a ride; micro-timing (the
//                     snare laid back, the hats pushed, every hit humanised). Its kit: GFX DEEP's own analogue
//                     circuits on its DEEP CLASSIC kit (pinged resonators: a new hit adds to what still rings; an accent
//                     bus; band-limited metal), through its drum bus (compressor, EQ, tape, reverb): the kick; the snare /
//                     clap voice (the clap on the backbeats, the snare for ghost notes and the flurries); the closed and
//                     open hats (one voice, two VCAs: the closed chokes the open); the rim. Its missing lanes made from
//                     its circuits: the toms are its kick retuned (96 / 142 Hz, shorter, less drive), the ride (and the
//                     crash after a fill) a hat voice of their own, lower and long.
//              PULSE  relentless: the kick on every group and quarter, hats and percussion spread Euclidean (k hits
//                     as evenly as they go over the bar, turned by SYNCOPATION), off-beat open hats, the off-beat
//                     sixteenths accented as SYNCOPATION rises; every player leans forward. Its kit: GFX PULSE's eight
//                     voices (ANALOG: its circuit engine - pinged resonators, the accent bus, the shared hat metal) on its
//                     own note order (kick, snare, closed / open hat, clap, tom; the high tom its tom a
//                     fourth up; the cymbal lane its perc, its cymbal for the hit after a fill), on CHARACTER (a drop-
//                     down under the keys: ANALOG by default, FM, PHYSICAL, DIGITAL).
//   METER      a drop-down: 3/4, 4/4, 6/4, 6/8, 12/8, 5/4, 5/8, 7/4, 7/8, 9/4, 9/8, 15/8, CUSTOM or 4/4 IN
//              TRIPLETS (twelve triplet eighths a bar); GROUPING (a
//              drop-down of the meter's groupings: 4/4 2+2+2+2, 3+3+2, 2+3+3 eighths; 5/4 3+2, 2+3, 2+2+2+2+2; 7/8 and
//              7/4 2+2+3, 3+2+2, 2+3+2; 9/8 2+2+2+3, 3+3+3, 3+2+2+2; 9/4 2+2+2+3, 3+3+3; 12/8 3+3+3+3, 3+3+2+2+2; 15/8
//              3+3+3+3+3, 2+2+2+3+3+3, 3+3+3+2+2+2 ...). The steps are sixteenths. The grouping drives the
//              generation: each step's weight (the bar's start, a group's start, its quarters, eighths, sixteenths),
//              the backbeats (the starts of the second, fourth .. groups, and the third of three), the kick's
//              anticipations, the open hat's off-beats, SPARSITY's rests and where the fills start. CUSTOM: STEPS a
//              bar (2 - 32) and the groups wherever you click the ring of numbers round the rose (an accent
//              pattern; step 1 always starts one). FOLLOW PROJECT (lit): REAPER's time signature instead (one from
//              the table takes its GROUPING; any other is grouped in quarters, or in dotted / plain eighths for
//              x/8; bars are counted from the project's start, so keep one signature).
//   GENERATION SEED (00001 - 99999; < > step it, the wheel too), NEW (a new seed and no edits; not while LOCK is
//              lit), LOCK (lit: the bar repeats exactly - no variation from bar to bar), CLEAR EDITS, VARIATION (how
//              much each bar re-rolls its choices: 0 = every bar the same), DENSITY (all lanes: 50 % = each engine's
//              own; it sets how many steps each lane plays - the best-scoring ones), SPARSITY (whole groups rest,
//              lane by lane; the kick's downbeat and the backbeats stay), SYNCOPATION (the weight moves off the beat:
//              anticipations, off-beat sixteenths, PULSE's Euclidean turn).
//   THE ROSE   the display: the lanes as rings of stained glass (kick at the centre, then snare, clap / rim, the toms,
//              the cymbal, the open and closed hats outermost), the steps as panes, the groups in gold tracery (the
//              bar's start the heaviest), the ring of numbers outside. A pane lights in its lane's jewel by velocity;
//              a ghost note lights only its inner half; a fill's steps carry gold beads (and a ratchet's ticks) and
//              the fill's steps glow rose on the ring; an edited step has a gold dot. The playhead is a beam of light;
//              the hub shows the meter, the grouping and the bar. Hover a pane for what it holds; click it to light or
//              darken it (drag to paint along a lane): the edit is kept in the project (and automation-safe hidden
//              sliders) and holds through every bar; right-click (or double-click) gives the pane back to the
//              generator.
//   LANES      kick, snare, closed hat, open hat, clap / rim, low tom, high tom, cymbal / perc: MUTE (lit: the lane
//              is silent - kit and MIDI), DENSITY (the lane's own, with the global), LEVEL, TUNE (+/- 12 semitones)
//              and DECAY (on each engine's own pitch and decay times; they take the next hit), NOTE OUT (a drop-down of every MIDI note with
//              the GM drum names; GM by default: 36, 38, 42, 46, 39, 45, 50, 49). Click a lane's name to hear it.
//   GROOVE     SWING (the off-beat sixteenths late: 100 % = a full triplet), GROOVE (the engine's micro-timing: none
//              for PCM12; DEEP's lay-back and humanising; PULSE's lean), ACCENT (the dynamics' contrast: 0 = all hits
//              equal, 50 % = as written, 100 % = doubled).
//   FILLS      WHEN (Off, every 2 / 4 / 8 / 16 bars, or BY CHANCE: CHANCE a bar), LENGTH (the last beat - the last
//              group -, the last half bar, the whole bar), INTENSITY. Each engine its own: PCM12 rolls down snare,
//              high tom, low tom (sixteenths, or eighths when gentle); DEEP flurries ghost notes (some doubled), a
//              rim on the group starts, an open hat; PULSE rushes the snare (ratchets, faster as it goes) and the hats,
//              the kick driving on. The bar after a fill opens with a cymbal. The next fill is shown.
//   VOICES     KIT (lit: the built-in kit plays; off, the Hierophant is a MIDI brain), MIDI OUT (lit: each hit as a
//              note on the lane's map, sample-accurate, velocity from the hit; each note ended after a short gate
//              and before the lane's next; every note ended when the run stops or jumps, MIDI OUT goes off or its
//              channel changes), CHANNEL (1 - 16, 10 by default), DRIVE (the house drive on the kit's bus: up to
//              +30 dB into the anti-aliased knee clipper, the drive-tracking pre-filter and knee, level-matched).
//              The kit: the running engine's voices, three a lane; a stolen voice fades in 4 ms, the closed hat
//              chokes the open hat (6 ms), an engine change fades the old engine's voices (10 ms); only the engine
//              in use (or one still ringing out) runs. MIDI in: notes on the
//              note map play the kit (and pass through); IN channel (Omni or 1 - 16) in the header.
//   TRANSPORT  RUN (lit) plays the pattern; it loads stopped and presets never start it. TRANSPORT (lit by
//              default): REAPER's play / record starts the run and its stop stops it; RUN still works by hand (free
//              at the project tempo from bar 1 while REAPER is stopped). While REAPER plays the run is locked to its
//              position: a bar starts where the song's bar starts, and a loop or a jump picks up where it lands.
//   PATTERNS   the EDITOR / PATTERNS keys in the header switch the card between the editor and THE LIBRARY: classic
//              drum-machine patterns style by style (Afro-Cuban to Waltz, numbered within each style; an "(A/B)" pattern
//              is two bars, A then B), the ENDINGS and the TIMELINES, five columns a page (< > or the wheel to page);
//              click one to load it, the one in force is lit, GENERATED (or GENERATE in GENERATION) gives the bar back
//              to the generator. A pattern takes its meter (4/4, 4/4 in triplets, 12/8, 3/4) and the neutral settings
//              (DENSITY 50 % and every lane's 50 %, VARIATION 0, SPARSITY 0, GROOVE 0, SWING 0, FATE 0, no edits, the
//              anchor off): there it plays exactly as written. The generator then works from it: DENSITY below 50 % drops
//              hits (never the downbeat kick, the backbeats or the accents), above adds hits where the engine likes
//              them (ghosts on the snare and hats), VARIATION re-rolls a few every bar, SPARSITY rests whole groups,
//              GROOVE the engine's micro-timing, FATE the congregation. Its fills are its style's breaks (the same meter,
//              chosen by the seed; WHEN and LENGTH as ever). Step edits work on it; CLEAR EDITS returns to it. Lanes: BD
//              the kick, SD the snare (DEEP: its snare), CH / OH the hats, CY the cymbal lane (crash or ride), HT the high
//              tom, MT the high tom a third down (PCM12: its TOM 2), LT the low tom, CP and RS the clap-rim lane (each its
//              own sound: the clap, the rim - PULSE's rim its perc, high and short), CB and TAM the cymbal / perc lane
//              (its cowbell, a tambourine); flams a soft grace note just before (PCM12 15 ms, DEEP 20, PULSE 12); the
//              accent row full velocity (each engine's accent), other hits at the engine's normal level. MIDI out: each
//              instrument its GM note (the lanes' map; the rim 37, the mid tom 47, the ride 51, the cowbell 56, the
//              tambourine 54). A TIMELINE (12/8 bells, 6/8, 5/8, 7/8, 9/8, 11/8, 13/8, 15/8, 4/4 claves and bells,
//              Euclidean figures, a 3/4 hemiola) sets its meter and grouping and plays on the anchor (a bell), the
//              generator writing the rest; the timelines are in the anchor's FIGURE drop-down too.
//   ANCHOR     the ninth row of THE CHOIR (and the cinnabar ring round the rose's hub): a steady figure that ignores
//              the seed, the densities, VARIATION and the fills, to anchor the generated lanes. Its lit key (ANCHOR)
//              turns it on; FIGURE: every beat (a quarter in x/4, a group in x/8), every eighth, every sixteenth, the
//              group starts, the off-beats, a clave (the grouping's groups plus one, spread as evenly as they go) or
//              the backbeat; SOUND from the engine's kit (shaker, tambourine, closed hat, cowbell / ride, rim / clap,
//              kick - PCM12 from its ROM; DEEP's shaker and tambourine are its hat circuit, noise-led or bright;
//              PULSE's a small voice of its own in its noise-and-metal style); ACCENT (the group starts louder),
//              LEVEL, its MIDI note (a new sound brings its GM note: 70 maracas, 54 tambourine, 42, 56 / 51, 37 /
//              39, 36), M (mute). It follows SWING and the engine's lean; FATE moves it only lightly (a steady player).
//   FATE       the series' drift (0 - 100 %, 50 % = the standard amount), the CONGREGATION: each lane is a player of
//              its own - its own lean (the snare a touch behind, the hats ahead) and dynamics - and each drifts
//              slowly along the song (a few ms, about 10 % in level), reckoned from the song position, so a render
//              still repeats. It also sets the engines' analogue spread and drift (each voice a little off its
//              tuning and decay, a warm-up, a slow wander, a touch of hit-to-hit variation; PCM12's clock tolerance
//              and wander): 50 % standard, 0 = exact and repeatable.
//   VOLUME, LEVEL, SAT (the output soft clip is always on).

import gfx-drift.jsfx-inc
import gfx-output.jsfx-inc
import gfx-ui.jsfx-inc
import gfx-panel.jsfx-inc
import gfx-instrument.jsfx-inc
import gfx-clip.jsfx-inc

